# State

## The cropper state

All cropping data lives in a single object, the **cropper state**. Components draw it, gestures and methods replace it, and nothing else carries information about the crop.

It has five parts: the boundary, the image size, the transforms, the visible area and the coordinates.

```ts
interface CropperState {
	boundary: Boundary;
	imageSize: ImageSize;
	transforms: Transforms;
	visibleArea: VisibleArea | null;
	coordinates: Coordinates | null;
}
```

By convention the state itself is `null` until the cropper has an image and a measured boundary. A state whose `visibleArea` and `coordinates` are both set is called _initialized_. Modifiers return an uninitialized state unchanged, and `isInitializedState(state)` tells the two apart.

<StateGeometry />

`visibleArea` and `coordinates` are both rectangles measured in **image pixels**, in the frame of the rotated image. Only `boundary` is measured in screen pixels.

### Boundary

The boundary is the size of the cropper's drawing area on screen, in CSS pixels.

```ts
interface Boundary {
	width: number;
	height: number;
}
```

### Image size

The natural size of the image, before any rotation is applied.

```ts
interface ImageSize {
	width: number;
	height: number;
}
```

The relation between image pixels and screen pixels matters a lot. If the visible area is twice as wide as the boundary, dragging the stencil one screen pixel moves the coordinates by two image pixels. The core calls this ratio the _coefficient_ (`getCoefficient(state)`, which is `visibleArea.width / boundary.width`) and uses it to convert every pointer delta before applying it.

### Visible area

The part of the image that is currently displayed inside the boundary.

```ts
interface VisibleArea {
	top: number;
	left: number;
	width: number;
	height: number;
}
```

Because the visible area is stretched to fill the boundary, its aspect ratio always matches the boundary's. Zooming the image in makes the visible area smaller, and panning moves it.

### Coordinates

The crop itself: the part of the image the stencil covers, and the region `getCanvas()` draws.

```ts
interface Coordinates {
	top: number;
	left: number;
	width: number;
	height: number;
}
```

### Transforms

The rotation and flips applied to the image.

```ts
interface Transforms {
	rotate: number;
	flip: {
		horizontal: boolean;
		vertical: boolean;
	};
}
```

`rotate` is in degrees and accumulates: rotating by 90° four times gives `360`, not `0`. See the [FAQ](/docs/faq#how-to-get-the-default-state) for why that matters when you reset the cropper.

## Reading the state in Svelte

The cropper instance (from `bind:this`, or the argument of any callback) exposes the state and its parts:

| Method                    | Returns                                                            |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `getState()`              | A deep copy of the whole state, or `null`                          |
| `getCoordinates()`        | The coordinates, rounded to whole pixels by default                |
| `getVisibleArea()`        | A copy of the visible area                                         |
| `getTransforms()`         | A copy of the transforms (no rotation and no flips when empty)     |
| `getStencilCoordinates()` | The stencil's box in boundary (screen) pixels                      |

All of them are reactive, so you can use them directly in markup:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Cropper, type CropperRef } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let { src }: { src: string } = $props();
	let cropper: CropperRef | undefined = $state();

	const coordinates = $derived(cropper?.getCoordinates());
</script>

<Cropper bind:this={cropper} {src} />
{#if coordinates}
	<p>{coordinates.width} × {coordinates.height} at ({coordinates.left}, {coordinates.top})</p>
{/if}
```

To replace the state, pass a new state, a partial state, or a function of the current state to `cropper.setState()`. See [Modifiers](/docs/concept/modifiers).
